WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.372
Begin by framing Foundations of Object
Identity and State as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:08.372 --> 00:00:10.750
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:18.312
A class declares state and behavior, while an
object is an instance with its own runtime

4
00:00:18.312 --> 00:00:20.203
identity and field state.

5
00:00:20.203 --> 00:00:27.446
Consider this worked case: A `User` instance
maintains its own `username` and

6
00:00:27.446 --> 00:00:31.671
`sessionToken` values independent of other
`User` instances.

7
00:00:31.671 --> 00:00:38.482
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

8
00:00:38.482 --> 00:00:38.968
conclusion.

9
00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:44.765
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

10
00:00:44.882 --> 00:00:52.860
Now define Inheritance versus Composition for
Reuse precisely enough that another learner

11
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.519
could test the definition.

12
00:00:55.519 --> 00:01:03.214
Inheritance allows a class to derive behavior
from base classes, while composition connects

13
00:01:03.214 --> 00:01:05.582
objects through explicit attributes.

14
00:01:05.582 --> 00:01:13.187
Consider this worked case: A `Vehicle` base
class provides `move()` logic, but a

15
00:01:13.187 --> 00:01:19.623
`DeliveryTruck` composes a `CargoContainer`
object instead of inheriting from `Truck` to

16
00:01:19.623 --> 00:01:21.378
avoid rigid hierarchies.

17
00:01:21.378 --> 00:01:27.692
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

18
00:01:27.692 --> 00:01:29.988
evidence would distinguish them.

19
00:01:29.988 --> 00:01:36.628
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

20
00:01:36.746 --> 00:01:43.494
With the foundation in place, examine how
Dynamic Method Dispatch and Polymorphism

21
00:01:43.494 --> 00:01:46.305
operates in a concrete sequence.

22
00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:53.153
Inheritance reuses and specializes behavior,
but dynamic method dispatch selects an

23
00:01:53.153 --> 00:01:58.133
implementation from the runtime class of the
receiver.

24
00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:05.024
Consider this worked case: Calling `draw()` on
a `Shape` reference invokes `Circle.

25
00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.961
draw()` or `Square.

26
00:02:06.961 --> 00:02:10.602
draw()` depending on the actual object at
runtime.

27
00:02:10.602 --> 00:02:18.133
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

28
00:02:18.133 --> 00:02:23.930
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

29
00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:31.017
Next, compare the choices involved in
Encapsulation and Access Control and make the

30
00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:32.625
decision criteria explicit.

31
00:02:32.625 --> 00:02:37.968
Access modifiers control which declarations
are visible.

32
00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:44.336
Consider this worked case: A `BankAccount`
class exposes `deposit()` and `withdraw()`

33
00:02:44.336 --> 00:02:50.703
methods while keeping the `balance` field
private to prevent unauthorized modification.

34
00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:58.002
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

35
00:02:58.002 --> 00:02:59.218
the goalposts.

36
00:02:59.218 --> 00:03:06.062
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

37
00:03:06.062 --> 00:03:13.094
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

38
00:03:13.094 --> 00:03:15.203
throughout the analysis.

39
00:03:15.314 --> 00:03:22.342
Apply Exceptions for Error Handling to a
worked situation instead of treating it as an

40
00:03:22.342 --> 00:03:23.279
isolated definition.

41
00:03:23.279 --> 00:03:28.185
Exceptions separate normal results from abrupt
completion.

42
00:03:28.185 --> 00:03:34.754
Consider this worked case: A `NetworkRequest`
throws a `ConnectionTimeoutException` when a

43
00:03:34.754 --> 00:03:41.920
server fails to respond, allowing the caller
to handle the failure gracefully.

44
00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:49.566
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

45
00:03:49.566 --> 00:03:50.076
step.

46
00:03:50.076 --> 00:03:55.092
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

47
00:03:55.202 --> 00:04:00.845
Examine the tradeoffs in Generics for
Type-Safe Abstractions, including what

48
00:04:00.845 --> 00:04:04.230
improves and what becomes more difficult.

49
00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:11.331
Generic types express reusable abstractions
while preserving compile-time type

50
00:04:11.331 --> 00:04:12.120
constraints.

51
00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:19.845
Consider this worked case: A `Queue` class
ensures that only elements of type `T` are

52
00:04:19.845 --> 00:04:24.480
added or removed, eliminating the need for
runtime casting.

53
00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:31.089
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

54
00:04:31.089 --> 00:04:38.573
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

55
00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:45.979
Stress-test the reasoning around Event-Driven
Programming Principles with an edge case and

56
00:04:45.979 --> 00:04:49.624
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

57
00:04:49.624 --> 00:04:56.088
This course covers advanced concepts such as
event-driven programming, design patterns,

58
00:04:56.088 --> 00:05:02.552
distributed and concurrent programming, and
the use, design and implementation of

59
00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:03.140
applications.

60
00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:10.597
Consider this worked case: A `UIController`
registers a `Button` instance to emit `click`

61
00:05:10.597 --> 00:05:15.186
events, triggering a `EventHandler` without
direct method calls.

62
00:05:15.186 --> 00:05:21.858
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

63
00:05:21.858 --> 00:05:29.264
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

64
00:05:29.378 --> 00:05:36.592
Use Design Patterns for Structural Integrity
to diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

65
00:05:36.592 --> 00:05:38.812
causes, and corrective action.

66
00:05:38.812 --> 00:05:43.453
Python objects have identity, a type, and a
value.

67
00:05:43.453 --> 00:05:50.097
Consider this worked case: The `Strategy`
pattern allows a `PaymentProcessor` to swap

68
00:05:50.097 --> 00:05:56.187
`CreditCard` and `Crypto` algorithms at
runtime without modifying the core class.

69
00:05:56.187 --> 00:06:03.817
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

70
00:06:03.817 --> 00:06:05.452
smallest plausible cause.

71
00:06:05.452 --> 00:06:12.827
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

72
00:06:12.938 --> 00:06:19.064
Connect Concurrent and Distributed Programming
Foundations to the earlier ideas and explain

73
00:06:19.064 --> 00:06:22.638
how the pieces work as one system.

74
00:06:22.638 --> 00:06:29.388
Attribute lookup follows defined rules across
an instance and its class hierarchy.

75
00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:37.001
Consider this worked case: A `TaskScheduler`
uses thread pools to process independent jobs

76
00:06:37.001 --> 00:06:43.443
concurrently, ensuring that shared resources
are accessed via synchronized locks or

77
00:06:43.443 --> 00:06:45.200
immutable data structures.

78
00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:54.910
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

79
00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:56.528
across representations.

80
00:06:56.528 --> 00:07:03.809
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

81
00:07:03.914 --> 00:07:09.823
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist for
Implementation as a practical checklist that

82
00:07:09.823 --> 00:07:12.285
can guide a new analysis.

83
00:07:12.285 --> 00:07:18.675
Object-oriented design should make
responsibilities, invariants, dependencies,

84
00:07:18.675 --> 00:07:23.239
composition, and substitution behavior
explicit.

85
00:07:23.239 --> 00:07:30.021
Consider this worked case: Before deploying a
`DataProcessor`, developers check that each

86
00:07:30.021 --> 00:07:37.368
class has a single responsibility, validates
that internal state cannot be corrupted, and

87
00:07:37.368 --> 00:07:43.020
confirms that subclasses can replace base
classes without breaking contracts.

88
00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:50.969
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

89
00:07:50.969 --> 00:07:52.192
verification step.

90
00:07:52.192 --> 00:07:58.900
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

91
00:07:58.900 --> 00:07:59.379
intuition.
